• The Glitch: Typo overflow. When adding the ULL suffix to -1 and +1 bit offsets on a fully saturated 64-bit pattern (0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE), additional hex characters were typed onto the end of the string instead of performing standard hexadecimal arithmetic. This pushed the literal values past the 64-bit threshold, causing Clang to reject the binary structure.
• The Correction: The bit values have been strictly bound back down to exactly 16 hex characters. For the -1 Bit test, ...EEEE drops cleanly to ...EEED. For the +1 Bit test, adding a bit to 0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE is implemented by shifting and setting the lowest available bit position to change the dense pattern footprint safely without exceeding register width limits.
• The Problem: A typographical count overflow. Adding an extra character (D or F) to the end of a full 16-character string (0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE) creates a 68-bit literal. Clang flags this immediately because it overflows standard machine hardware register constraints.
• The Arithmetic Correction: To drop the base matrix by exactly 1 bit cleanly within a 64-bit register boundary, the 16th hex character itself must change.
	• Base Pattern: 0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEE (16 digits)
	• Correct -1 Bit: 0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEF? No, E (binary 1110) minus 1 is D (binary 1101). So it becomes: 0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEED (exactly 16 digits).
	• Correct +1 Bit: E (binary 1110) plus 1 is F (binary 1111). So it becomes: 0xEEEEEEEEEEEEEEEF (exactly 16 digits).

